Q: data.table Cell Capping and Cumulative Row Budgeting A: <p>There are two parts. Your first part 1 is fine as is but I found it slightly more efficient to use <code>pmin()</code> directly and skip the <code>i</code> argument.</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code># First add L for integer
num_rows  &lt;- 1e6L
num_cols  &lt;- 12L 
ind_limit &lt;- 20L
agg_limit &lt;- 100L


for (col in col_names) set(dt2, j = col, value = pmin(dt2[[col]], ind_limit))
</code></pre>
<p>Converting each row to 100 according to your logic best belongs in a function of its own and it would be much simpler to work with a matrix in Rcpp rather than data.table. One example</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>Rcpp::cppFunction(&quot;void finish100_mat(IntegerMatrix X, int upper = 100) {
  int nrow = X.nrow(), ncol = X.ncol();
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; nrow; i++) {
    int cur_total = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; ncol; j++) {
      cur_total += X(i, j);
      if (cur_total &gt;= upper) {
        X(i, j) -= cur_total - upper;
        for (int k = j + 1; k &lt; ncol; k++) X(i, k) = 0;
        break;
      }
    }
  }
}&quot;)

# Convert to matrix
dt2m &lt;- as.matrix(dt2)
finish100_mat(dt2m)

# Check everything is correct
library(checkmate)
assert_double(rowSums(dt2m), upper = 100, lower = 0)
assert_true(max(dt2m) == 20L, min(dt2m) &gt;= 0L)

# Now convert back to data.table
dt2 &lt;- as.data.table(dt2m)
&gt; head(dt2, 3)
#        A     B     C     D     E     F     G     H     I     J     K     L
#    &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;
# 1:    20     7    20    20    20     9     4     0     0     0     0     0
# 2:    20    20     0    14    20    20     6     0     0     0     0     0
# 3:     6    20     7    20     0    20    14    13     0     0     0     0
</code></pre>

Q: How to deal with DateTimeOffset handling between SQL Server and Sqlite when using unmapped type in EF Core A: <p>Not sure if this will help because I am working on an old not EF system. Had a similar problem with DATEDIFF(day, ...) because sqlite presumes day is a column and fails.</p>
<p>So for testing I intercept the datediff function in the sql and replace day with 'day', this then allows the datediff function to work. Know these are different functions and different tech (EF vs Sql string) but it might give you an idea.</p>
<pre><code>    private IDbConnection CreateConnection()
    {
        var connection = new SqliteConnection(_connection.ConnectionString);
        connection.Open();
        // only does day. for other datePart options capture the datePartObject _ and add a switch
        connection.CreateFunction(&quot;DATEDIFF&quot;, (object _, string startDateStr, string endDateStr) =&gt; DateTime.TryParse(startDateStr, out var startDate) &amp;&amp; DateTime.TryParse(endDateStr, out var endDate)
                ? (int)(endDate - startDate).TotalDays
                : 0
        );
        return new SqliteTestDbConnection(connection);
    }
</code></pre>
<p>In the DbCommands I replace datediff(day with datediff('day'</p>
<pre><code>    public SqliteTestDbCommand(SqliteConnection connection)
    {
        _inner = connection.CreateCommand();
    }

    public string CommandText
    {
        get =&gt; _inner.CommandText;
        set =&gt; _inner.CommandText = TransformSqlIfNeeded(value);
    }

    private string TransformSqlIfNeeded(string sql) {
        var hasDateDiff = Regex.IsMatch(sqlLowerCase, @&quot;datediff\(\s*day,&quot;);
        if (hasDateDiff)
        {
            sql = TransformDateDiff(CleanupSql());
        }
    }

    private string TransformDateDiff(string sql)
    {
        /*
    select datediff(day, a, b)
        */

        var selectQualifyPattern = new Regex(@&quot;datediff\(day,&quot;, RegexOptions.IgnoreCase);
        sql = selectQualifyPattern.Replace(sql, &quot;datediff('day',&quot;);
        return sql;
    }

</code></pre>
<p>Hope this help somewhat.</p>
